    "body": "Plain sailing for CMA CGM’s shipping business, but an uphill climb for Ceva. AI-generated image\nBy Alex Lennane\n16 November 2026\nCMA CGM’s shipping profits surged in the third quarter – but its expanding logistics business earned less on more revenue, underlining the task facing Ceva’s new management.\nFor context, Maersk generated $519m of logistics EBITDA on $4.49bn of revenue, compared with CMA CGM’s $366m on $5.46bn.\nAlthough the pair have different business mixes and acquisitions, Ceva enters its latest restructuring with CMA’s logistics division growing faster – and earning less than its Danish rival.\nAcross the group, CMA CGM’s revenue rose 31.1%, to $18.4bn, EBITDA increased 61.6%, to $4.78bn, and net profit more than tripled, to $2.32bn.\nShipping carried the improvement: volumes increased 5.5%, to 6.51m teu, and revenue per teu rose 30%, to $1,891. Shipping EBITDA reached $4bn, with a margin of 32.4%.\nChairman and chief executive Rodolphe Saadé said: “We are determined to pursue our investments to strengthen our market positions and keep providing innovative solutions to our customers.”\nCMA identified restocking, AI infrastructure, and Chinese clean-technology and electric vehicle exports as demand drivers. It expects strong volumes and freight rates to persist, but warned of geopolitical and trade policy uncertainty.\nA source close to the group told AFP that companies, particularly in the US, had been rebuilding inventories since Q2 as a precaution against conflict, oil-price, and tariff uncertainty. The source also pointed to the industrial components needed to build data centres as a source of container demand.\nIn logistics, revenue increased 19.2%, but EBITDA declined 14.4%, reducing the margin from 9.3% to 6.7%. CMA attributed the pressure to “difficult forwarding conditions and automotive sector weakness”.\nAcquisitions also supported the logistics division’s expansion. AFP identified contributions from Borusan, Fagioli, and Fattal’s distribution businesses, now branded Tamina, although their individual contributions were not disclosed.\nMaersk’s Logistics & Services division, by comparison, increased revenue by 12.7% and EBITDA by 10.9%. Its EBITDA margin remained broadly stable, at 11.6%, against 11.7% a year earlier. Forwarding improved its EBIT margin, from 3.5% to 4.3%, supported by strong air and project logistics volumes – something of a counterpoint to CMA’s description of forwarding market difficulties, although the companies have different exposures.\nFor Ceva, the quarter provides the starting point for CEO Patrick Moebel’s overhaul . As The Loadstar Premium previously reported , Mr Moebel told senior leaders that “clear pain points” were slowing the business and contributing to performance below industry standards and internal expectations.\nThe proposed changes consolidate activities around freight management and contract logistics, strengthen regional accountability, and move decisions closer to customers. Future organisation and reporting lines were to be implemented no later than 1 January 2027, subject to applicable consultation processes.\nMr Moebel’s ambition is to generate more than $1bn of “additional value” over three years, although the financial measure behind that target remains unclear.\nThere is more integration work ahead. CMA CGM completed its $1.4bn acquisition of FedEx Supply Chain on 1 October, adding a further business that did not contribute to the latest results.\nThe deal also involved aircraft moving in the opposite direction. Cargo Facts reported on 11 November that FedEx had acquired two CMA CGM 777 freighters as part of the transaction. A FedEx spokesperson linked the purchase to its MD-11F replacement strategy.\nCMA’s quarterly update describes eight aircraft operating under its Air Cargo and Air Belgium brands, supported by hubs in France, Belgium and the US.\nStandalone air cargo financial results were not disclosed. The Air Cargo and Terminals division contributed to growth in “other activities”, where revenue increased 27.1%, to $1.51bn, and EBITDA rose 41.9%, to $413m. CMA cited the solid momentum of both businesses.\nFedEx Supply Chain’s contribution will begin to appear in Q4, while the operating overhaul is due by January. Subsequent results will show whether Ceva can accompany its next increase in scale with a recovery in profitability.\nCheck out today’s News in Brief podcast , on the latest developments in US tariffs and customs enforcement, what a Democrat-controlled Congress could mean for the US trade agenda – as well as air freight updates.",
